"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdocs=self.webpackChunkdocs||[]).push([["7358"],{91895(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"hacktoberfest-2026-recap","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/hacktoberfest-2026-recap","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/edit/dev/docs/blog/2026-11-08-hacktoberfest-2026-recap/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-hacktoberfest-2026-recap/index.md","title":"Hacktoberfest 2026: 28 first-time contributors, 46 merged pull requests \u{1F994}","description":"The Img2Num Hacktoberfest 2026 recap: what 28 first-time contributors shipped in 46 merged pull requests across the C++ core, the GPU path, the Python and JavaScript packages, the example apps, the docs and CI - and where to collect your badge.","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Hacktoberfest","permalink":"/blog/tags/hacktoberfest","description":"Hacktoberfest events, issues, and participation"},{"inline":false,"label":"Community","permalink":"/blog/tags/community","description":"Community news, events, and highlights"},{"inline":false,"label":"Open Source","permalink":"/blog/tags/open-source","description":"Open-source development and the wider ecosystem"},{"inline":false,"label":"Contributing","permalink":"/blog/tags/contributing","description":"How to contribute, good first issues, and contributor guides"}],"readingTime":12.17,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ryan Millard","title":"Img2Num Author","url":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/ryan-millard"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/ryan-millard"},"imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/142347829?v=4","key":"ryan-millard"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Hacktoberfest 2026: 28 first-time contributors, 46 merged pull requests \u{1F994}","description":"The Img2Num Hacktoberfest 2026 recap: what 28 first-time contributors shipped in 46 merged pull requests across the C++ core, the GPU path, the Python and JavaScript packages, the example apps, the docs and CI - and where to collect your badge.","keywords":["img2num","hacktoberfest","hacktoberfest 2026","open source","first-time contributors","contributor badge","image to svg","webgpu","python gil","typescript types"],"image":"./img/social.webp","slug":"hacktoberfest-2026-recap","authors":["ryan-millard"],"tags":["hacktoberfest","community","open-source","contributing"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Contribute to Img2Num this October \u{1F994}","permalink":"/blog/hacktoberfest-2026"}},"content":"import ThemedImage from \\"@theme/ThemedImage\\";\\nimport useBaseUrl from \\"@docusaurus/useBaseUrl\\";\\n\\n![Hero](./img/social.webp)\\n\\nAt the end of September we [asked you to contribute to Img2Num this Hacktoberfest](/blog/hacktoberfest-2026), and we were\\nupfront about the deal: Hacktoberfest 2026 stopped counting pull requests, so there was no counter to climb and no\\nt-shirt at the end of it. A reviewed and merged pull request in a real project was the whole prize and\\n\\n<strong>twenty-eight of you took it!</strong>\\n\\nBetween 3 and 30 October we merged **46 pull requests from 28 contributors**. None of the contributors had contributed to\\nImg2Num before October. They fixed output bugs in the C++ core, made the Python bindings release the GIL,\\ngave the npm package TypeScript types, rebuilt pages of this site, and deleted more than 1 300 lines of code that was no longer\\nneeded. Every one of them is now listed on the [Hacktoberfest 2026 event page](/events/hacktoberfest-2026/), along with\\ntheir pull requests.\\n\\nThis post is the long version that explains what got built, what it changed, and what happens next.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## The numbers\\n\\n- **28 contributors**, every one of them merging into Img2Num for the first time.\\n  [GitHub\'s contributor graph for October](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/graphs/contributors?from=2026-10-01&to=2026-10-31&type=c)\\n  has the same names.\\n- **46 pull requests merged** between 3 and 30 October\\n  ([the full list on GitHub](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pulls?q=is%3Apr%20is%3Amerged%20merged%3A2026-10-01..2026-10-31%20-author%3ARyan-Millard%20-author%3Akrasner%20-author%3APrachi-Gupta2808)).\\n  Maintainers\' own pull requests (five, including the new CLI) are not counted.\\n- **177 files changed, <span style={{ color: \\"var(--ifm-color-success)\\" }}>+5 232</span>, and <span style={{ color: \\"var(--ifm-color-danger)\\" }}>\u22122 959</span> lines.**\\n- **At least 29 issues closed** by those pull requests.\\n- **Under four days** from opening a pull request to merging it, at the median. Twelve merged within a day; 37 of the\\n  46 within a week.\\n- **10 contributors opened more than one pull request.** Three of them, [@Lothnic](https://github.com/Lothnic),\\n  [@saptadeep12](https://github.com/saptadeep12), and [@nitin-kumar-code](https://github.com/nitin-kumar-code) opened five each.\\n\\n## What got built\\n\\nThis section groups the changes according to where they were made in the project. Pull request numbers link to GitHub, and the\\n[event page](/events/hacktoberfest-2026/) has the same work grouped by person.\\n\\n### C++ core and GPU (8)\\n\\n- **A reusable WebGPU helper layer** - [#658](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/658) by\\n  [@siddharth1407](https://github.com/siddharth1407). RAII wrappers over Dawn (`gpu::Buffer<T>`, `gpu::Texture`,\\n  bind-group and compute-pass helpers), then both GPU kernels ported onto them: the bilateral filter went from 313\\n  lines to 120 with identical behaviour. [#694](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/694) documented the new\\n  layer the same week.\\n- **Two more ways to fall back to the CPU** - [#663](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/663) by\\n  [@ayusharora07](https://github.com/ayusharora07) stops the WebGPU device being initialised twice and falls back when\\n  device creation fails; [#696](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/696) by\\n  [@BhoomiMahna](https://github.com/BhoomiMahna) does the same when the only adapter Dawn can find is the Null backend.\\n  A machine without a usable GPU now gets the CPU path instead of a failed conversion.\\n- **Correct output at the edges** - [#659](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/659) by\\n  [@RahulKumarBaldia](https://github.com/RahulKumarBaldia). `gaussian_blur_fft` now uses reflect padding, which fixes\\n  darkened borders and content wrapping around from the opposite edge.\\n- **k-means with k = 1** - [#682](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/682) by\\n  [@nitin-kumar-code](https://github.com/nitin-kumar-code) fixed the CPU path\'s output when there is only one cluster.\\n  A two-line change; wrong output before, right output after.\\n- **Fewer run-to-run differences** - [#765](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/765) by\\n  [@saptadeep12](https://github.com/saptadeep12) orders each graph node\'s neighbours by id, so the contour graph no\\n  longer depends on the order they happened to be inserted in.\\n- **`ptr` became `image`** - [#749](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/749) by\\n  [@MiyaMango](https://github.com/MiyaMango) renamed the parameter across the C++ core, the C ABI and the JavaScript\\n  glue. Small, and the API reference reads better now!\\n- **157 lines of unreachable contour-tracing code removed** -\\n  [#768](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/768) by\\n  [@shivangmathur2008-stack](https://github.com/shivangmathur2008-stack), with the matching internal docs.\\n\\nMost of this shipped in [cpp 0.3.0](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/releases/tag/cpp-v0.3.0) and\\n[bindings-c 0.2.1](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/releases/tag/bindings-c-v0.2.1) on 23 October.\\n\\n### Python (6)\\n\\n- **The GIL is released while the C++ core runs** - [#777](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/777) by\\n  [@Lothnic](https://github.com/Lothnic). Python threads that call Img2Num now run the native work in parallel instead\\n  of taking turns, and the console example gained a two-worker `ThreadPoolExecutor` demo to prove it. [Krasner](https://github.com/Krasner)\'s\\n  [#784](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/784) built on it a week later to make the GPU path parallel too.\\n- **`img2num.__version__`** - [#758](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/758), and **Python 3.13** declared\\n  in the package classifiers - [#754](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/754), both by\\n  [@Lothnic](https://github.com/Lothnic).\\n- **Typos in config are errors now** - [#766](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/766) by\\n  [@IvanGiovany](https://github.com/IvanGiovany): an unknown key in `ImageToSvgConfig` raises `TypeError`.\\n- **A one-line fix for a double move** - [#665](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/665) by\\n  [@Gracy769](https://github.com/Gracy769) removed a `std::move` that moved the SVG string twice in `labels_to_svg`.\\n- **Wheels carry only the Python component** - [#767](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/767) by\\n  [@Farhan-Alam-Jamal](https://github.com/Farhan-Alam-Jamal) scoped the CMake install to the `python` component, so\\n  wheels stop picking up files that belong to other parts of the build.\\n\\nNone of this is on PyPI yet - the current release is 0.3.1. It ships with the next Python release, together with the\\nnew [`img2num` command-line tool](/docs/py/cli) that [Krasner](https://github.com/Krasner) built in\\n[#703](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/703) and [@Adithya6721](https://github.com/Adithya6721) documented\\nin [#801](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/801).\\n\\n### JavaScript and C (2)\\n\\n- **TypeScript declarations for the npm package** - [#670](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/670) by\\n  [@saptadeep12](https://github.com/saptadeep12). The declarations are generated from tightened JSDoc with\\n  `tsc --allowJs --declaration`, so the JSDoc stays the single source of truth; `types` comes first in every `exports`\\n  branch; and a strict-mode `tsc --noEmit` type test runs in CI so they cannot drift. Ships in the next npm release.\\n- **The C error API got real prototypes** - [#764](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/764) by\\n  [@Ayushrawatcy009](https://github.com/Ayushrawatcy009). In C, `img2num_get_last_error()` declares a function taking\\n  unspecified arguments; `img2num_get_last_error(void)` is a prototype. Shipped in bindings-c 0.2.1.\\n\\n### Example apps (7)\\n\\n- The React example\'s **Vitest suite runs again** - [#713](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/713) by\\n  [@Lothnic](https://github.com/Lothnic). It also lost **1,370 lines** of About and credits pages that had nothing to do\\n  with the library - [#789](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/789) by\\n  [@mrunalC27](https://github.com/mrunalC27); its `/credits` redirect now points at the [Hall of Fame](/hall-of-fame/) -\\n  [#793](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/793) by\\n  [@slopgobblercobbler](https://github.com/slopgobblercobbler); and it has a README -\\n  [#660](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/660) by [@PrathamRanka](https://github.com/PrathamRanka).\\n- The console apps: the Python one **validates its image input** -\\n  [#661](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/661) by [@PrathamRanka](https://github.com/PrathamRanka); the C++\\n  one was **tidied up** - [#687](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/687) by\\n  [@saptadeep12](https://github.com/saptadeep12); and the Node ones (ESM and CJS) have a **correct usage message and an\\n  output-path option** - [#677](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/677) by\\n  [@Astrolyte](https://github.com/Astrolyte).\\n\\n### Docs and this website (15)\\n\\n- **The Hall of Fame** - [#679](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/679) by\\n  [@08abhinav](https://github.com/08abhinav). The page that honours contributors was itself built by a first-time\\n  contributor: a data file of featured members, GitHub profile and merged-PR data fetched at build time, bot filtering,\\n  and fallbacks so an API hiccup cannot break the build. The [events pages](/events/) sit on top of it.\\n- **The Showcase** - [#644](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/644) by\\n  [@dr-nullptr](https://github.com/dr-nullptr) turned the example-apps index into [/showcase](/showcase/), with a\\n  \\"Built with Img2Num\\" section for third-party projects and a [submission page](/showcase/submit) that opens a\\n  prefilled Discussion.\\n- **The FAQ** redesigned with accordions - [#623](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/623) by\\n  [@flodrick](https://github.com/flodrick); **Core Concepts became a glossary** -\\n  [#646](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/646) by [@FathimaManal](https://github.com/FathimaManal);\\n  **Discord** in the navbar, footer and contributing guide - [#689](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/689)\\n  by [@saptadeep12](https://github.com/saptadeep12); **site styles and the navbar blur** fixed -\\n  [#680](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/680) by [@thilakshan2003](https://github.com/thilakshan2003);\\n  **\\"Edit this page\\" links point at `dev`** - [#770](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/770) by\\n  [@satyaharendrarushidayaka-sudo](https://github.com/satyaharendrarushidayaka-sudo).\\n- **Docs that match the code**: `min_thickness` defaults documented -\\n  [#655](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/655) by [@Yipjunwei](https://github.com/Yipjunwei);\\n  `black_threshold_image` described as the per-channel brightness cutoff it is, in the C++ header, the Python docstring\\n  and the docs - [#692](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/692) by [@Lothnic](https://github.com/Lothnic); the\\n  `gpu_utils` layer documented - [#694](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/694) by\\n  [@siddharth1407](https://github.com/siddharth1407); the Debug Logging guide rewritten to match the actual code -\\n  [#771](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/771) by [@Ayushrawatcy009](https://github.com/Ayushrawatcy009);\\n  the swapped C and JavaScript rows in the API bindings tables -\\n  [#762](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/762) by\\n  [@mayanksingh-7974](https://github.com/mayanksingh-7974); the synchronous-API migration guidance in our own blog\\n  post corrected - [#760](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/760) by\\n  [@jazayush](https://github.com/jazayush); what happens when several people claim one issue, in the contributing\\n  guide - [#795](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/795) by [@saptadeep12](https://github.com/saptadeep12);\\n  and the new [CLI guide](/docs/py/cli) - [#801](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/801) by\\n  [@Adithya6721](https://github.com/Adithya6721).\\n\\n### Build, CI and tooling (8)\\n\\n- **CMake pinned** in `.versions/cmake.json`, with the minimum version derived from it -\\n  [#666](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/666) by\\n  [@Farhan-Alam-Jamal](https://github.com/Farhan-Alam-Jamal).\\n- **A Docker build command** in the dev script - [#669](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/669) by\\n  [@thisisparth187](https://github.com/thisisparth187).\\n- **The justfile split into modules** - [#695](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/695) by\\n  [@mrunalC27](https://github.com/mrunalC27), with the console binary paths corrected afterwards -\\n  [#750](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/750) by [@Yipjunwei](https://github.com/Yipjunwei).\\n- **Stray dependencies removed** from the root and dev-scripts packages -\\n  [#759](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/759), and `validate-scripts` handling an optional\\n  `_meta.ignore` - [#774](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/774), both by\\n  [@mayanksingh-7974](https://github.com/mayanksingh-7974).\\n- **CI cancels superseded runs** when you push again to an open pull request -\\n  [#757](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/757) by [@nitin-kumar-code](https://github.com/nitin-kumar-code).\\n- **111 lines of dead release workflow removed** - [#799](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/799) by\\n  [@mrunalC27](https://github.com/mrunalC27).\\n\\n## The badge\\n\\n<ThemedImage\\n  alt=\\"Img2Num contributor \u2014 Hacktoberfest 2026\\"\\n  width=\\"240\\"\\n  height=\\"240\\"\\n  sources={{\\n    light: useBaseUrl(\\"/img/events/hacktoberfest-2026/badge.svg\\"),\\n    dark: useBaseUrl(\\"/img/events/hacktoberfest-2026/badge-dark.svg\\"),\\n  }}\\n/>\\n\\nIf your name is on the [event page](/events/hacktoberfest-2026/), this is yours. It comes as SVG and PNG in light and\\ndark versions, with ready-made Markdown and HTML snippets for a README or a profile, and a credential URL you can paste\\ninto LinkedIn\'s _Licenses & certifications_. Everything is at the top of the\\n[event page](/events/hacktoberfest-2026/).\\n\\nHacktoberfest did not count pull requests toward rewards this year. We thought 28 first merged pull requests deserved\\nsomething you can keep.\\n\\n## How it went from our side\\n\\nA few things we noticed reviewing 46 pull requests in a month:\\n\\n- **The first three days set the pace.** Fourteen pull requests were opened on 1\u20133 October, on top of three that had\\n  been waiting since the last days of September. By 11 October, 20 had merged.\\n- **Small and specific merged fastest.** Twelve pull requests merged within a day of being opened, and each of them\\n  did one clearly described thing. A good title and a `Fixes #...` line are most of the work of getting reviewed\\n  quickly.\\n- **Big changes landed too.** The GIL release, the TypeScript declarations and the WebGPU helper layer were each\\n  several hundred lines, and all three changed what the library can do. This was not a month of typo fixes.\\n- **Deleting is contributing.** More than a third of the changed lines were deletions. The biggest pull request of the\\n  month by lines, [#789](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/789), removed 1,370 of them.\\n- **People stayed.** Ten of the 28 opened more than one pull request.\\n\\nThanks to [Krasner](https://github.com/Krasner), who reviewed and co-authored several of these and shipped the CLI\\n([#703](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/703)) and truly parallel GPU execution\\n([#784](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/784)) in between, and to\\n[Prachi](https://github.com/Prachi-Gupta2808), who wrote [the post that started it](/blog/hacktoberfest-2026) and\\nposted about it on [LinkedIn](https://lnkd.in/p/g4Xh3KbQ).\\n\\n## What\'s next\\n\\n- **Releases.** cpp 0.3.0 and bindings-c 0.2.1 are out with the core and C work. The Python and JavaScript work\\n  above - the GIL release, `__version__`, the `TypeError`, Python 3.13, the TypeScript declarations and the CLI - ships\\n  in the next PyPI and npm releases.\\n- **The October cohort is not done.** [@BhoomiMahna](https://github.com/BhoomiMahna)\'s\\n  [#702](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/pull/702), adding C++ and C ABI test coverage, merged on 4 November -\\n  the first post-Hacktoberfest pull request from someone who started in October. We hope for far more!\\n- **Discord.** Contributors get the Contributor role and the private channel. Post your GitHub username in\\n  [Discord](https://discord.gg/BHjxcCqAnU) and we will match it to the list.\\n- **You do not have to wait for next October.** The\\n  [good first issues](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/issues/views/MDI0OlJlcG9zaXRvcnlTZWFyY2hTaG9ydGN1dDExNTU%3D)\\n  are still there, and so are we.\\n\\nThank you, all 28 of you. \u{1F994}"},{"id":"hacktoberfest-2026","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/hacktoberfest-2026","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/edit/dev/docs/blog/2026-09-30-hacktoberfest-2026/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-30-hacktoberfest-2026/index.md","title":"Contribute to Img2Num this October \u{1F994}","description":"It\'s [Hacktoberfest](https://hacktoberfest.com/) season! Here\'s how to make your first (or next) open source contribution to Img2Num, and where to start.","date":"2026-09-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Hacktoberfest","permalink":"/blog/tags/hacktoberfest","description":"Hacktoberfest events, issues, and participation"},{"inline":false,"label":"Open Source","permalink":"/blog/tags/open-source","description":"Open-source development and the wider ecosystem"},{"inline":false,"label":"Community","permalink":"/blog/tags/community","description":"Community news, events, and highlights"},{"inline":false,"label":"Contributing","permalink":"/blog/tags/contributing","description":"How to contribute, good first issues, and contributor guides"}],"readingTime":5.7,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Prachi Gupta","title":"Img2Num Contributor","url":"https://github.com/Prachi-Gupta2808","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/prachi-gupta"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/Prachi-Gupta2808","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/prachi-gupta-74122a324/"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/Prachi-Gupta2808.png","key":"prachi-gupta"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"hacktoberfest-2026","title":"Contribute to Img2Num this October \u{1F994}","authors":["prachi-gupta"],"tags":["hacktoberfest","open-source","community","contributing"],"description":"It\'s [Hacktoberfest](https://hacktoberfest.com/) season! Here\'s how to make your first (or next) open source contribution to Img2Num, and where to start.","image":"./img/social.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Hacktoberfest 2026: 28 first-time contributors, 46 merged pull requests \u{1F994}","permalink":"/blog/hacktoberfest-2026-recap"},"nextItem":{"title":"Img2Num JS 0.4.2: One npm Package, Five Purpose-Built WebAssembly Bundles","permalink":"/blog/img2num_js_0_4_2"}},"content":"![Hero](./img/social.png)\\n\\n<p style={{ fontSize: \\"2em\\" }}>October is [Hacktoberfest](https://hacktoberfest.com/) season, and we\'d love for this to be the month you contribute to **Img2Num**!</p>\\n\\n> Whether you\'re a seasoned open source contributor or someone making their very first pull request, we\'d love to have you\\n> join us.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## What is [Hacktoberfest](https://hacktoberfest.com/)?\\n\\n:::tip[Summary]\\n\\n[Hacktoberfest](https://hacktoberfest.com/) is a month-long celebration of open source software that runs every October. It started in 2014 as a pull-request\\nchallenge, and for over a decade it introduced thousands of developers to open source.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n**This year is a bit different.** [Hacktoberfest](https://hacktoberfest.com/) 2026, run by Major League Hacking (MLH) and DEV with DigitalOcean, has moved\\naway from counting pull requests and toward in-person and online events focused on building with open source AI. The\\nreason is one every maintainer will recognise:\\n\\n> low-effort, AI-generated PRs were flooding projects with noise\\n\\nSo to be clear: **contributions to Img2Num won\'t count toward anything official this year.** No PR counter, no t-shirt.\\nBut October is still a great month to start contributing to open source, and we\'re still here to help you do it.\\n\\nWe agree with the spirit of the change, too. One well-thought-out and meaningful contribution is worth far more than ten low-effort AI-generated ones.\\n\\n## Where Img2Num fits in\\n\\nThis year\'s [Hacktoberfest](https://hacktoberfest.com/) theme is **AI belongs to everyone**. Img2Num isn\'t an LLM or agent project, but it lives in\\nthe same world: it\'s built on classical computer vision and machine learning, including k-means clustering for colour\\nquantization, plus contour tracing and skeletonization.\\n\\nIf you\'re curious about how machines actually make sense of images, before any neural network gets involved, this is a\\ngreat place to learn.\\n\\n## Why contribute to Img2Num?\\n\\nImg2Num is a fast, accurate raster-to-SVG vectorization library built in C++ with library support for C++, C, Python, and JavaScript. It is\\nactively used, actively maintained, and actively growing - all thanks to our users and contributors.\\n\\nHere\'s what makes contributing to Img2Num worthwhile for **you**:\\n\\n:::note[Real experience]\\nYou\'ll work with C and C++, JavaScript and WebAssembly, Python, React, and Docusaurus. These are technologies used in production systems.\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[CV/resume material]\\nOpen source contributions are one of the best ways to demonstrate your skills to future employers.\\nA merged PR in a real project is worth more than most personal projects.\\n:::\\n\\n:::warning[Mentorship]\\nWe actively mentor newcomers. You will not be left alone to figure things out. We review PRs carefully and explain our feedback.\\n\\nIf you get stuck along the way, give us a shout on [Discord](https://discord.gg/BHjxcCqAnU) - someone\'s usually around.\\n:::\\n\\n:::danger[Long-term community]\\nOctober is just the start. Some of our maintainers started with a single small PR.\\n:::\\n\\nAnd here\'s what your contributions give **us**:\\n\\n- More work done. The project moves faster with more hands - there\'s always something that needs to be done.\\n- Fresh perspectives. Newcomers often spot things that long-time contributors overlook.\\n- Long-term contributors. Some of the best team members started as first-timers.\\n\\n## New to open source? We\'ve got you covered.\\n\\n> First of all: **THANK YOU!**\\n>\\n> Even the smallest contributions can be enormously helpful and the fact that you\'re\\n> considering contributing to an open source project is amazing.\\n\\nIf this is your first time exploring open source, you might be wondering where to even begin.\\n\\nWe know exactly how difficult and confusing it can feel when you start contributing to open source. There are unfamiliar\\nrepositories, contribution guidelines, issues, pull requests, branches, reviews, and a lot of new terminology to figure\\nout. Your first contribution can feel much more complicated than it actually is.\\n\\nThat\'s why we\'ve created a bunch of\\n[**Good First Issues specifically for newcomers**](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/issues/views/MDI0OlJlcG9zaXRvcnlTZWFyY2hTaG9ydGN1dDExNTU%3D).\\nYou don\'t need to be an open source expert to get started,\\nand you don\'t need to know everything before making your first contribution.\\n\\n:::note[Good First Issues]\\n\\nFind small bugs, documentation updates, and isolated features curated for new contributors.\\n\\n<a href=\\"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/issues/views/MDI0OlJlcG9zaXRvcnlTZWFyY2hTaG9ydGN1dDExNTU%3D\\" target=\\"_blank\\" rel=\\"noopener noreferrer\\" style={{ display: \\"flex\\", justifyContent: \\"center\\" }}>\\n  View Issues on GitHub \u2192\\n</a>\\n\\n:::\\n\\nStart with an issue that interests you, read the contributing guide, and ask questions whenever you\'re stuck. We\'ll help\\nyou understand the project and the contribution process along the way.\\n\\nAnd don\'t worry if your first contribution is something small. Fixing documentation, improving an example, adding a\\ntest, fixing a typo, or making a small improvement is still a real contribution that we need!\\n\\n**If you\'re new to open source, we\'ve got you covered. \u{1F994}**\\n\\nCheck out our\\n[Good First Issues](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/issues/views/MDI0OlJlcG9zaXRvcnlTZWFyY2hTaG9ydGN1dDExNTU%3D)\\nand find something that feels approachable.\\n\\n## Beyond Img2Num\\n\\nWhile you\'re in the open source spirit, we\'d also encourage you to look beyond Img2Num.\\n\\nImg2Num is built on top of and alongside several amazing open source projects. These projects provide important building\\nblocks and baseline functionality that help make Img2Num possible.\\n\\nIf you enjoy working on Img2Num, you might also find something interesting to contribute to in the projects we depend\\non:\\n\\n- [**Dawn**](https://dawn.googlesource.com/dawn). Google\'s WebGPU implementation, which powers our GPU acceleration.\\n- [**spdlog**](https://github.com/gabime/spdlog). The fast C++ logging library we use.\\n- [**STB**](https://github.com/nothings/stb). Single-file C libraries used in our example apps.\\n- [**Pybind11**](http://github.com/pybind/pybind11). The library that allows us to make our Python library possible.\\n- [**Emscripten**](https://emscripten.org/). The compiler toolchain that converts our C++ code into WebAssembly, which\\n  powers our JavaScript library.\\n- [**Docusaurus**](https://github.com/facebook/docusaurus). The framework that powers our documentation site.\\n- [**OpenCV**](https://github.com/opencv/opencv). Used in our Python example app.\\n\\nThese projects are a small part of the open source ecosystem that Img2Num is built with and around. They have their own issues,\\ndocumentation, tests, features, and communities that can benefit from new contributors too.\\n\\n> There are many more open source projects that have made Img2Num possible. If you contribute towards improving one of\\n> them, you\'re basically improving Img2Num.\\n\\nSo if you find yourself thinking, \\"I want to contribute to open source, but maybe Img2Num isn\'t the right project for\\nme,\\" that\'s completely fine.\\n\\nExplore these projects, find something that interests you, and make your first contribution there.\\n\\nEvery contribution to any open source project makes the ecosystem better for everyone.\\n\\n## See you in October \u{1F994}\\n\\nWe\'re excited to meet you. Whether you fix a typo or implement a feature, your contribution matters.\\n\\nCome say hello on [our Discord server](https://discord.gg/BHjxcCqAnU) for quick questions and chat, or open a thread in\\n[our Discussions](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/discussions) for ideas, proposals, and anything worth keeping\\nsearchable. Then pick up an issue, and let\'s build something great together."},{"id":"img2num_js_0_4_2","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/img2num_js_0_4_2","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/edit/dev/docs/blog/2026-08-19-img2num_js_0_4_2/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-08-19-img2num_js_0_4_2/index.md","title":"Img2Num JS 0.4.2: One npm Package, Five Purpose-Built WebAssembly Bundles","description":"Img2Num 0.4.2 ships five purpose-built WASM bundles on npm - browser ESM, IIFE, UMD, and Node ESM/CJS - fixing CommonJS require() crashes and bundler .wasm resolution for image-to-SVG conversion in JavaScript.","date":"2026-08-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Release","permalink":"/blog/tags/release","description":"New version releases and changelogs"},{"inline":false,"label":"JavaScript","permalink":"/blog/tags/javascript","description":"Posts about the JavaScript/npm package"},{"inline":false,"label":"WebAssembly","permalink":"/blog/tags/wasm","description":"WebAssembly builds and browser usage"}],"readingTime":11.72,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ryan Millard","title":"Img2Num Author","url":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/ryan-millard"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/ryan-millard"},"imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/142347829?v=4","key":"ryan-millard"},{"name":"Ayush Jaiswal","title":"Img2Num Contributor","url":"https://github.com/jazayush","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/ayush-jaiswal"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/jazayush","linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/jazayush/"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/jazayush.png","key":"ayush-jaiswal"}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Img2Num JS 0.4.2: One npm Package, Five Purpose-Built WebAssembly Bundles","description":"Img2Num 0.4.2 ships five purpose-built WASM bundles on npm - browser ESM, IIFE, UMD, and Node ESM/CJS - fixing CommonJS require() crashes and bundler .wasm resolution for image-to-SVG conversion in JavaScript.","keywords":["img2num","image to svg","raster to svg","javascript svg converter","webassembly","wasm","npm","commonjs","esm","vectorization"],"image":"./img/social.png","slug":"img2num_js_0_4_2","authors":["ryan-millard","ayush-jaiswal"],"tags":["release","javascript","wasm"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Contribute to Img2Num this October \u{1F994}","permalink":"/blog/hacktoberfest-2026"},"nextItem":{"title":"We\'re Expanding the Maintainer Team","permalink":"/blog/img2num-maintainer-expansion"}},"content":"import Link from \\"@docusaurus/Link\\";\\nimport Tabs from \\"@theme/Tabs\\";\\nimport TabItem from \\"@theme/TabItem\\";\\n\\n![Hero](./img/social.png)\\n\\nImg2Num\'s [JavaScript package v0.4.2](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num) is out on npm, and it\'s the biggest change to\\nthe npm distribution since we first shipped WebAssembly builds.\\n\\nIn short, instead of one compromise bundle, the package now ships five purpose-built bundles,\\neach backed by its own Emscripten glue variant, and getting started no longer requires\\nknowing anything about Emscripten - regardless of whether you\'re in a bundled web app,\\na plain `<script>` tag, or Node.js.\\n\\n**If you\'re new here:** Img2Num is a C++-based library for converting raster images into clean SVGs, with bindings for C, Python, and JavaScript.\\nThis release is about the [JavaScript](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num) and WebAssembly side of the library - the\\n[C++ core](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/releases?q=cpp),\\n[C API](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/releases?q=bindings-c), and\\n[Python package](https://pypi.org/project/img2num/) are unchanged.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n:::warning[Why 0.4.2 and not 0.4.0?]\\n\\nBecause our example apps kept catching bugs faster than we could write this post.\\n\\n[**0.4.0**](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.0) shipped everything described below - plus two latent bugs, one per environment.\\nIn the browser, the published wasm URL wasn\'t statically detectable by bundlers, so bundled\\napps (Vite, webpack, Rollup) hit a 404 on `img2num.wasm` at runtime. Our own React example\\ncaught it the same day, and 0.4.0 is now [deprecated on npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.0).\\n\\n[**0.4.1**](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.1) fixed the browser build - but CommonJS was still broken. `require(\\"img2num\\")`\\ncrashed on Node < 22.12 with `ERR_REQUIRE_ESM` instead of converting: our load of the\\nESM-only [`webgpu`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/webgpu) package was compiled into a\\ntop-level `require()` call, and when that failed, the promised CPU fallback died too -\\nthe Emscripten glue dereferences `navigator`, which doesn\'t exist in Node, so the\\nconversion aborted with `navigator is not defined` instead of falling back. Node >= 22.12\\nsupports `require()` of ES modules and masked all of this, which is exactly why our dev\\nmachines never saw it. Our new Node CJS example caught it two days later.\\n\\n[**0.4.2**](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.2) is the release as intended, and it exists specifically to make CommonJS work:\\nthe `webgpu` load is now a real dynamic `import()` in the CJS output, and the fallback\\npath installs a stub `navigator.gpu` so the glue genuinely routes to the CPU. Both bug\\nclasses now have build-time guards that inspect the _emitted_ bundles and fail the build\\nif either regression ever reappears - because both times, the source was correct and the\\npublished artifact wasn\'t.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Why we rebuilt the build\\n\\nUntil now, the npm package shipped a single Emscripten build that tried to work everywhere.\\nIt mostly did - but \\"mostly\\" meant subtle path-resolution bugs when bundlers relocated the `.wasm` file,\\nawkward CJS/ESM interop in Node, and a `<script>`-tag story that was harder than it should have been (it was basically unusable).\\n\\nThe underlying problem is that one Emscripten glue _cannot_ serve every consumption mode.\\nES6-style glue emits top-level `await` and `import.meta`, which UMD and IIFE chunks can\'t carry.\\nCJS-shaped glue reads `__dirname`, which ESM doesn\'t have. Web glue drags XHR branches into Node;\\nNode glue drags `fs`/`path` branches into the browser.\\nNo single artifact can be all of these at once, so we stopped trying to make that idea work.\\n\\n[0.4.2](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/releases/tag/packages-js-v0.4.2)\\nbuilds three WASM variants (web, standalone (iife/umd), node) through a parameterized CMake function,\\nthen emits five bundles from per-target Vite configs:\\n\\n| Bundle                                                                                                          | Format | WASM             | Use case                                  |\\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\\n| [`dist/browser/img2num.js`](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/browser/img2num.js)                 | ESM    | External `.wasm` | Bundled web apps (Vite, webpack, Rollup)  |\\n| [`dist/standalone/img2num.iife.js`](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/standalone/img2num.iife.js) | IIFE   | Inlined          | Plain `<script>` tag, CDNs                |\\n| [`dist/standalone/img2num.umd.js`](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/standalone/img2num.umd.js)   | UMD    | Inlined          | AMD/RequireJS and legacy loaders          |\\n| [`dist/node/img2num.js`](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/node/img2num.js)                       | ESM    | External `.wasm` | Modern Node projects (`import`)           |\\n| [`dist/node/img2num.cjs`](https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/node/img2num.cjs)                     | CJS    | External `.wasm` | Existing CommonJS codebases (`require()`) |\\n\\nYour package manager and bundler pick the right one automatically via the `exports` map -\\nyou just `import { ... } from \\"img2num\\"` (or `require` it) and it\'ll work.\\n\\nThe WASM strategy differs by target on purpose.\\nThe standalone IIFE/UMD builds inline the binary, so a `<script>` tag loads one file with no path resolution at all.\\nThe browser ESM and Node builds ship a real sibling `.wasm` file that your bundler resolves and copies via `new URL(..., import.meta.url)` -\\nwe had to actively defeat Vite\'s lib-mode habit of inlining it as a data URL, which cut the browser entry from ~900 kB to ~108 kB.\\nThat tug-of-war is exactly what bit [0.4.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.0): our anti-inlining trick also hid the URL from _your_ bundler.\\n[0.4.1](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.1) fixed it by mangling the URL only during our build and restoring the bundler-detectable literal in the published output,\\nso both sides of the fight now win - and a build guard fails loudly if the literal ever goes missing again.\\n\\nThe Node builds fight a different interop battle - the one that forced [0.4.2](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.2). The optional\\nGPU path loads the [`webgpu`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/webgpu) package, which is\\nESM-only - so the CJS bundle has to load it with a dynamic `import()`, the one form of ESM\\nloading that works inside CommonJS everywhere. [0.4.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.0) and [0.4.1](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.1) shipped that as a\\ncompiled-in `require()` (see the warning above); [0.4.2](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.4.2) ships the real `import()`, and if\\n`webgpu` is missing or fails to load for any reason, Img2Num now installs a stub\\n`navigator.gpu` so the WASM glue genuinely falls back to the CPU instead of crashing on\\n`navigator is not defined`. A matching build guard scans the emitted CJS chunk and fails\\nthe build if any executable `require(\\"webgpu\\")` ever sneaks back in.\\n\\n## How to use Img2Num in the browser and Node.js\\n\\nThe same two calls - decode to pixels, convert to SVG - work in every environment.\\nHere\'s the shortest working version of each target, with a live sandbox to poke at:\\n\\n<Tabs groupId=\\"js-target\\">\\n<TabItem value=\\"browser-esm\\" label=\\"Browser ESM\\" default>\\n\\nFor bundled apps (Vite, webpack, Rollup) or a native `<script type=\\"module\\">` - the `exports` map resolves the browser build automatically:\\n\\n```js\\nimport { imageToUint8ClampedArray, imageToSvg } from \\"img2num\\";\\n\\nconst fileInput = document.querySelector(\\"#fileInput\\");\\n\\nfileInput.addEventListener(\\"change\\", async (e) => {\\n  const { pixels, width, height } = await imageToUint8ClampedArray(e.target.files[0]);\\n  const { svg } = await imageToSvg({ pixels, width, height });\\n\\n  document.querySelector(\\"#preview\\").src = \\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\\" + btoa(svg);\\n});\\n```\\n\\n**[Try it on CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/p/sandbox/delicate-bird-3kprj2)**\\n\\n</TabItem>\\n<TabItem value=\\"iife\\" label=\\"IIFE\\">\\n\\nOne plain `<script>` tag, one global `Img2Num` object - the WASM is inlined, so there\'s nothing else to serve:\\n\\n```html\\n<script src=\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/standalone/img2num.iife.js\\"><\/script>\\n<script>\\n  const { imageToUint8ClampedArray, imageToSvg } = Img2Num;\\n\\n  fileInput.addEventListener(\\"change\\", async (e) => {\\n    const { pixels, width, height } = await imageToUint8ClampedArray(e.target.files[0]);\\n    const { svg } = await imageToSvg({ pixels, width, height });\\n    preview.src = \\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\\" + btoa(svg);\\n  });\\n<\/script>\\n```\\n\\n**[Try it on CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/p/sandbox/iife-fh25gg)**\\n\\n</TabItem>\\n<TabItem value=\\"umd\\" label=\\"UMD (RequireJS)\\">\\n\\nLoaded as an AMD module - nothing is added to the global scope:\\n\\n```html\\n<script src=\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/requirejs@2.3.8/require.js\\"><\/script>\\n<script>\\n  requirejs.config({\\n    paths: { img2num: \\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/standalone/img2num.umd\\" },\\n  });\\n\\n  requirejs([\\"img2num\\"], ({ imageToUint8ClampedArray, imageToSvg }) => {\\n    fileInput.addEventListener(\\"change\\", async (e) => {\\n      const { pixels, width, height } = await imageToUint8ClampedArray(e.target.files[0]);\\n      const { svg } = await imageToSvg({ pixels, width, height });\\n      preview.src = \\"data:image/svg+xml;base64,\\" + btoa(svg);\\n    });\\n  });\\n<\/script>\\n```\\n\\n**[Try it on CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/p/sandbox/competent-shape-2drsrz)**\\n\\n</TabItem>\\n<TabItem value=\\"node-esm\\" label=\\"Node ESM\\">\\n\\n`import` resolves the Node ESM build. Decode with whatever you like - here, [sharp](https://www.npmjs.com/package/sharp):\\n\\n```js\\nimport { writeFileSync } from \\"fs\\";\\nimport { imageToSvg, terminateWasmModule } from \\"img2num\\";\\nimport sharp from \\"sharp\\";\\n\\nconst { data, info } = await sharp(\\"input.png\\").ensureAlpha().raw().toBuffer({ resolveWithObject: true });\\n\\nconst pixels = new Uint8ClampedArray(data.buffer, data.byteOffset, data.byteLength);\\n\\ntry {\\n  const { svg } = await imageToSvg({ pixels, width: info.width, height: info.height });\\n  writeFileSync(\\"output.svg\\", svg);\\n} finally {\\n  await terminateWasmModule();\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**[Try it on CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/p/devbox/node-esm-jmn444)**\\n\\n</TabItem>\\n<TabItem value=\\"node-cjs\\" label=\\"Node CJS\\">\\n\\n`require()` resolves the CJS build - the one this release exists to fix. No flags, no interop wrappers:\\n\\n```js\\nconst { writeFileSync } = require(\\"fs\\");\\nconst { imageToSvg, terminateWasmModule } = require(\\"img2num\\");\\nconst sharp = require(\\"sharp\\");\\n\\nasync function main() {\\n  const { data, info } = await sharp(\\"input.png\\").ensureAlpha().raw().toBuffer({ resolveWithObject: true });\\n\\n  const pixels = new Uint8ClampedArray(data.buffer, data.byteOffset, data.byteLength);\\n\\n  try {\\n    const { svg } = await imageToSvg({ pixels, width: info.width, height: info.height });\\n    writeFileSync(\\"output.svg\\", svg);\\n  } finally {\\n    await terminateWasmModule();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nmain().catch(console.error);\\n```\\n\\n**[Try it on CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/p/devbox/node-cjs-dp5ltr)**\\n\\n</TabItem>\\n</Tabs>\\n\\n## Breaking changes\\n\\nThis is a majorish release for a reason, though migration is small for most codebases:\\n\\n:::danger[`dist/` layout and filenames have changed.]\\n\\n- If you were deep-importing into `dist/`, update those paths to the table above.\\n- If you import the package root, you don\'t have to change anything.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::danger[Export conditions are reordered.]\\n\\nThe `browser` condition now comes first, so bundlers targeting the browser actually get the browser build -\\npreviously they matched `import` first and silently received the Node build.\\nIf your bundle got smaller and your weird workaround stopped being necessary, that\'s why.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::danger[Minimum supported Node is now v18.]\\n\\nPreviously we documented Node >= 14; the new node-glue builds require version 18.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::note[Other Improvements]\\n\\nTwo quieter improvements ride along:\\n\\n1. The optional `webgpu` dependency in Node is now properly guarded:\\n   if it\'s missing (`--ignore-optional`), fails to install on your platform, or can\'t\\n   be loaded, Img2Num logs a warning and falls back to CPU instead of throwing.\\n   (0.4.0 and 0.4.1 claimed this and didn\'t deliver it - the fallback path itself crashed.\\n   0.4.2 delivers it, verified against both the ESM and CJS console examples with\\n   `webgpu` removed.)\\n\\n2. Initial WASM memory **dropped from 2 GB to 32 MB** - a 2 GB\\n   reservation fails outright on mobile Safari and bought nothing with memory growth enabled.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## What happened to the Web Worker?\\n\\nThat was last release.\\n[0.3.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.3.0) removed the internal Web Worker, so every call now runs on the thread that makes it -\\nImg2Num does the math, you decide where it runs.\\nThe functions are still `async`, though, so **keep your `await`**: without it you get a `Promise` back instead of a result.\\nIf you\'re moving from an earlier version straight to [v0.4.2](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num),\\nthe [0.3.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.3.0)\\nmigration still applies: if you need to keep the main thread free, wrap calls in your own worker:\\n\\n```js\\n// worker.js\\nimport { imageToSvg } from \\"img2num\\";\\n\\nself.onmessage = async ({ data }) => {\\n  const { pixels, width, height } = data;\\n  const { svg } = await imageToSvg({ pixels, width, height });\\n  self.postMessage(svg);\\n};\\n```\\n\\n[v0.4.2](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num) is what that change unlocked:\\nwith no worker plumbing forced into every artifact, each target could finally get a build shaped for its actual environment.\\n\\n:::tip[Why did [0.3.0](https://www.npmjs.com/package/img2num/v/0.3.0) remove the workers?]\\n\\nCross-environment compatibility. A hidden worker only made sense in some targets, and it\\nfought any worker architecture you already had. Running on the calling thread works everywhere -\\nImg2Num does the math, and you decide which thread it runs on.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Example apps for every binding\\n\\nTalking about build targets is abstract, so the examples got a matching overhaul:\\n\\n- `console-js` is now split into <a href=\\"/showcase/#node-esm\\"><code>console-js-esm</code></a> and <a href=\\"/showcase/#node-cjs\\"><code>console-js-cjs</code></a>,\\n  so the `import` and `require` export conditions are each exercised by a real consumer (and we fixed a byte-offset bug in the pixel-buffer construction while we were in there).\\n- The `html-js` example was rebuilt as a single shared template compiled into three self-contained variants -\\n  <Link to=\\"/example-apps/esm/\\" target=\\"_blank\\">\\n    ESM\\n  </Link>\\n  &comma;&nbsp;\\n  <Link to=\\"/example-apps/iife/\\" target=\\"_blank\\">\\n    IIFE\\n  </Link>\\n  &comma; and&nbsp;\\n  <Link to=\\"/example-apps/umd/\\" target=\\"_blank\\">\\n    UMD\\n  </Link>\\n  &nbsp;- each deployable as plain static files with no build tooling at runtime.\\n- The docs site now has an [example apps index](https://img2num.dev/showcase/) with a card and representative snippet for every binding:\\n  React, browser ESM, IIFE, UMD, Node ESM/CJS - and Python, C++, and C, because Img2Num was never just a JavaScript library.\\n- Every JS target is also runnable in your browser right now - the CodeSandbox links in the\\n  [tabs above](#how-to-use-img2num-in-the-browser-and-nodejs) are one click from \\"processed image\\" with nothing installed.\\n\\nEach example is intentionally minimal: the shortest path from \\"empty directory\\" to \\"processed image\\" in your environment of choice.\\nThey\'re also our first line of defense - and this release proved it twice.\\nThe React example caught the 0.4.0 browser bug before almost anyone could hit it,\\nand the new Node CJS example caught the CommonJS bug that survived into 0.4.1 the same way.\\nEvery entry point now has a real consumer that runs it the way you would.\\n\\n## Getting started\\n\\n```bash title=\\"Using npm\\"\\nnpm install img2num\\n```\\n\\n```html title=\\"Using a CDN (the CDN default resolves to the single-file IIFE build)\\"\\n<script src=\\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/img2num@0.4.2/dist/standalone/img2num.iife.js\\"><\/script>\\n```\\n\\n(Pin the version - your future self will thank you.)\\n\\n## What\'s next\\n\\nPurpose-built targets give us a clean baseline for the work we\'ve wanted to do for a while:\\nbenchmarking across environments and exploring WebGPU acceleration beyond the current optional Node support.\\nIf you hit anything odd with the new builds - an environment we didn\'t test, an interop edge case -\\nplease [open an issue](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/issues) so we can fix it for you.\\nReports from real setups are exactly what a release like this needs.\\n\\nThanks for using Img2Num! \u{1F994}\u{1F994}"},{"id":"img2num-maintainer-expansion","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/img2num-maintainer-expansion","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/edit/dev/docs/blog/2026-05-02-maintainers-expansion/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-05-02-maintainers-expansion/index.md","title":"We\'re Expanding the Maintainer Team","description":"Img2Num is expanding its maintainer team across C++, JavaScript, CI/CD, bindings, and documentation. Learn about available roles, maintainer levels, and how to apply.\\n","date":"2026-05-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Maintainers","permalink":"/blog/tags/maintainers","description":"Updates from maintainers and openings"}],"readingTime":2.33,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ryan Millard","title":"Img2Num Author","url":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/ryan-millard"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/ryan-millard"},"imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/142347829?v=4","key":"ryan-millard"}],"frontMatter":{"title_meta":"Img2Num is Expanding its Maintainer Team","slug":"img2num-maintainer-expansion","authors":["ryan-millard"],"tags":["maintainers"],"keywords":["Img2Num maintainers","open source maintainer roles","C++ project contributors","JavaScript open source project","CI/CD GitHub Actions","Docusaurus blog"],"description":"Img2Num is expanding its maintainer team across C++, JavaScript, CI/CD, bindings, and documentation. Learn about available roles, maintainer levels, and how to apply.\\n","image":"./2026-05-02-maintainers-expansion.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Img2Num JS 0.4.2: One npm Package, Five Purpose-Built WebAssembly Bundles","permalink":"/blog/img2num_js_0_4_2"},"nextItem":{"title":"Welcome to Img2Num!","permalink":"/blog/launch"}},"content":"![Img2Num maintainer expansion](./2026-05-02-maintainers-expansion.png)\\n\\nImg2Num has grown well past the point where one or two people can own it responsibly.\\n\\nThe codebase now spans a performance-critical C++ core, cross-language bindings (Python, JavaScript, C), a WebAssembly build pipeline, CI/CD automation, and a Docusaurus docs site. Keeping all of that high-quality and sustainable requires real ownership \u2014 not just occasional PRs.\\n\\nWe\'re looking for experienced contributors ready to take on scoped, ongoing responsibility.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Architecture Overview\\n\\nImg2Num is a layered system. Here\'s how the pieces fit together:\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TD\\n    A[\\"core/<br/>(C++ Library)\\"] --\x3e B[\\"bindings/c/<br/>(C ABI Library)\\"]\\n    A --\x3e C[\\"bindings/py/<br/>(Python - pybind11)\\"]\\n    B --\x3e E[\\"bindings/js/<br/>(WebAssembly via Emscripten)\\"]\\n\\n    E --\x3e D[\\"packages/js/<br/>(JavaScript Library)\\"]\\n    C --\x3e F[\\"packages/py/<br/>(Python Library)\\"]\\n\\n    A --\x3e G[\\"External Consumers\\"]\\n    B --\x3e G\\n    D --\x3e G\\n    F --\x3e G\\n\\n    classDef core fill:#1f6feb,stroke:#1f6feb,color:#fff;\\n    classDef layer fill:#2ea043,stroke:#2ea043,color:#fff;\\n    classDef intermediary fill:#595959,stroke:#595959,color:#fff;\\n\\n    class A core;\\n    class B,D,F layer;\\n    class C,E intermediary;\\n```\\n\\n| Layer              | Path           | Notes                                        |\\n| ------------------ | -------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\\n| C++ Core           | `core/`        | Source of truth for all algorithms           |\\n| C ABI              | `bindings/c/`  | Stable ABI boundary; feeds the Wasm pipeline |\\n| WebAssembly        | `bindings/js/` | Built via Emscripten from the C ABI          |\\n| JavaScript package | `packages/js/` | Safe runtime wrapper over the Wasm module    |\\n| Python bindings    | `bindings/py/` | pybind11 directly on the C++ core            |\\n| Python package     | `packages/py/` | Exposes the native Python module             |\\n\\n## Open Maintainer Areas\\n\\n### CI/CD\\n\\n- GitHub Actions workflows, Docker builds, release pipelines, automated publishing.\\n\\n### C ABI (`bindings/c`)\\n\\n- Maintain the C ABI over the C++ core. Must stay compatible with external C consumers and the Wasm pipeline.\\n\\n### JavaScript / WebAssembly (`bindings/js`, `packages/js`)\\n\\n- Emscripten integration, browser and Node.js compatibility, pnpm workspace tooling, React example app.\\n\\n### Python (`bindings/py`, `packages/py`)\\n\\n- pybind11 bindings, uv workspace tooling, console example app.\\n\\n### Docs & DX (`docs/`, `doxygen/`)\\n\\n- Docusaurus site, Doxygen integration, tutorials, onboarding experience.\\n\\n### Releases & Packaging\\n\\n- Versioning, tagging, GitHub Releases, npm and Docker Hub publishing.\\n\\n### Testing & Validation\\n\\n- CI test coverage, linting/formatting enforcement, regression testing.\\n\\n### Internal Tooling (`scripts/`)\\n\\n- Cross-platform CLI utilities, developer automation, local workflow improvements.\\n\\n## Maintainer Tiers\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n    A[Junior Maintainer<br/>PR review & learning] --\x3e B[Scoped Maintainer<br/>Domain ownership]\\n    B --\x3e C[Core Maintainer<br/>Architecture & final decisions]\\n```\\n\\n| Tier       | Merge Access        | Responsibilities                                                      |\\n| ---------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| **Junior** | None                | Review small PRs, contribute within a defined scope                   |\\n| **Scoped** | Within their domain | Own a specific area \u2014 review, approve, maintain quality               |\\n| **Core**   | Broad               | Cross-domain architecture, final merge authority, long-term direction |\\n\\nTrust is earned through consistent contributions. Everyone starts scoped.\\n\\n## How to Apply\\n\\nReply to [#309](https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/discussions/309) with:\\n\\n- Which area(s) you want to own\\n- A short summary of relevant experience\\n- GitHub profile or projects (optional)\\n\\nWe\'ll follow up directly."},{"id":"launch","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/launch","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard/Img2Num/edit/dev/docs/blog/2025-12-05-launch.md","source":"@site/blog/2025-12-05-launch.md","title":"Welcome to Img2Num!","description":"We are excited to launch Img2Num, the interactive app that converts any image into a color-by-number template.","date":"2025-12-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Launch","permalink":"/blog/tags/launch","description":"New feature launches"},{"inline":false,"label":"Tutorial","permalink":"/blog/tags/tutorial","description":"Guides on how the app works"}],"readingTime":0.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ryan Millard","title":"Img2Num Author","url":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard","page":{"permalink":"/blog/authors/ryan-millard"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/ryan-millard"},"imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/142347829?v=4","key":"ryan-millard"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"launch","title":"Welcome to Img2Num!","authors":["ryan-millard"],"tags":["launch","tutorial"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"We\'re Expanding the Maintainer Team","permalink":"/blog/img2num-maintainer-expansion"}},"content":"We are excited to launch **Img2Num**, the interactive app that converts any image into a color-by-number template.\\n\\n:::note[Edit: 20 June 2026]\\n\\nImg2Num is no longer an application for converting images into color-by-number templates.\\nInstead, it is now a library that supports C, C++, Python, and JavaScript.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nTry it out online at [https://img2num.dev/example-apps/react-js/](https://img2num.dev/example-apps/react-js/) and start coloring your favorite images today!\\n\\nStay tuned for tutorials, tips, and updates right here on the blog."}]}}')}}]);